0f"Peniophora-Yurchenko-2000.gif"Figure011313700
0 Dendrohyphidia present, brown immersed or hyaline and protruding the hymenium011413802
0 *. Dendrohyphidia absent011513903
0 Dendrohyphidia (dendrophyses) brown, immersed. Basidiomata when mature almost discoid (1—10 mm diam.), margin adnate, then elevated and rolling off. Hymenial surface grayish or with cream tinge. Lamprocystidia 42—62 x 15—23 µm. Subulate or bottle- like protruding cystidia present, smooth or loosely encrusted, 32—46 x A—6 µm. Basidia 30—34 x A—5 µm. Spores allantoid, 5—6(7) x (1.5)1.8—2(2.2) µm. On angiosperms (Malus) 024614100 Dendrophora versiformis (Berk. et M.A.Curtis) Chamuris (fig. 1).70
0The specimen is dose to D. erumpens (Burt.) Chamuris due to pezizaeform basidiomata breaking the bark(Boidin, 1994), but has more narrow spores (in D. erumpens 6—12 x 2—2.5 µm) and to consider as reflexed form of D. versiformis (see Davydkina, 1980). In our earlier account (Yurchenko, 1998) the specimen in question was mistakenly attributed to Peniophora aff. pilatiana Pouzar et Svrvcek due to very similar appearance.024714110N0
0 *. Dendrohyphidia of hymenial origin, abundant, encrusted, forming the layer above the hymenium. Margin adnate, then in some cases slightly reflexed. Hymenial surface pinkish or whitish with lilac or ochraceous tinge. Basal layer present. Hyphae with gelatinized walls. Gloeocystidia 25—40(60) x 11.5—21(22.5) µm, with gelatinized walls. Basidia 31—54x4.5—6 µm. Spores 6.5—10(11.5) x 3—3.5 µm, almost cylindrical or allantoid, frequently narrower toward the apex. On Populus tremula 024814130 P. polygonia (Pers.: Fr.) Bourdot et Galzin (fig. 2, 3).0
0 Gloeocystidia present0359151404
0 *. Gloeocystidia absent035101515012
0 On Carpinus betulus. Basidiomata decorticating (sometimes grow on bark surface), adnate; hymenium of hydnoid appearance with protruding hyphal pegs 0.2—3 mm high and 0.2—0.4 mm in diam., brownish yellow, ochraceous or pinkish. Basal layer indistinct. Gloeocystidia 40—44x6.5—9.5(10) µm. Lamprocystidia 17—31 x5—11 µm. Basidia 27—42x5—6 µm. Spores (6)8.5—12(14) x (2)2.5—4(5) µm, cylindrical or allantoid 0491116160 P. laeta (Fr.: Fr.) Donk.0
0 *. On different plants. Basidiomata not decorticating, sometimes found under separating bark, without hyphal hegs04912161705
0 All hyphae hyaline to yellowish, generally distinct. Sometimes brownish elements scarcely represented in the basal layer or in the thickened hymenium051213171806
0 *. Brownish or brown hyphae common, as a rule tightly united or forming pseudoparenchymatic structure051214171908
0 Spores large, ellipsoid, sometimes almost globose or narrowly ellipsoid, (10)12.5—19(22.5) x 8.5—11.5(12.5) µm. Hyphae clampless. Basidiomata effused. Hymenial surface cream-coloured, reddish yellow or orange. Basal layer present. Gloeocystidia 38—84(117) x 9—17.5 µm. Encrusted cystidia 33—86 x 5—8.5 µm, rare to abundant. Basidia 29—38 x 12—13.5 µm. On Alnus 06131518200 P. erikssonii Boidin (fig. 4).0
0 *. Spores narrower than 5 µm, hyphae clamped061316182107
0 Basidiomata wart-like, orange, pink, reddish, grayish violaceous, up to 2 mm thick and 1—5 mm in diam. Gloeocystidia broad, 50—120 x 10—20 µm, with gelatinized walls. Spores 5—7(10) x 2—2.5 µm, allantoid. Hyphae with gelatinized walls. Lamprocystidia 20—35 x 4—6 µm. Basidia 30—45 x 4—5 µm. On Populus tremula 07161719220 P. rufa (Fr.: Fr.) Boidin.0
0 *. Basidiomata commonly broadly effused, from 0.04—0.15 to 0.5—0.7 mm thick, cream, yellow, orange, pink, hazel yellow, sometimes whitish. Margin typically adnate or reflexed together with thin outer bark layer, sometimes distinctly free. Gloeocystidia narrower, 23—115(165) x (4.5)6—12.5 µm, walls not gelatinized. Spores (5)6.5—11(12.5) x (2.3)3—4(5) µm, narrowly ellipsoid to cylindrical, sometimes ellipsoid. Hyphae not gelatinized. Basal layer present or not. Some- times scarce brownish elements present near substrate or in thickened hymenium (MSK 4554). Protruding acute gloeocystidia and subulate cystidia with non oily contents sometimes present. Lamprocystidia (20)27—75 x 5.5—15(17) µm, abundant to very rare, sometimes absent. Basidia 25—38x4—7.5 µm. On different angiosperms (mainly Corylus, Frangula, Sorbus) rarely on Pinus 07161819230 P. incarnata (Pers.: Fr.) P. Karst.190
0Quite polymorphic taxon, could be more correctly named P. incarnata s. 1. or P. incarnata-complex. It varies in such taxonomic features as presence of reflexed margin, size and shape of basidiospores etc. However, to solve the problem of delimitation species in this complex the sexual incompatibility testing is required.07161919240N0
0 With elliptic or ovoid gloeocystidia, up to 18—25 µm wide081420202709
0 *. Gloeocystidia of other shape, up to 12—15 µm wide0814212028010
0 On Pinus sylvestris. Basal layer of hyaline hyphae with gelatinized walls well developed. Basidiomata orbiculare, 0.5—7 mm diam.; margin free. Hymenial surface gray or brown with violaceous or bluish tinge. Hymenium and subhymenium consist of brown elements with thickened walls. Gloeocystidia (12.5)15—63 x (7.5)12—22(25) µm, with gelatinized walls. Lamprocystidia rare or absent, 7—13.5 x5—9 µm. Basidia 27—36 x 4—6 µm. Spores allantoid, 5.5—7.5(9.5) x 2.3—3 µm 09202221290 P. pini (Schleich. et DC.: Fr.) Boidin. 0
0 *. On angiosperms (Malus, Syringa). Without well developed basal layer. Hyphal walls non gelatinized. Basidiomata effused, consists of brownish pseudoparenchymatic tissue. Hymenial surface pale, light ochraceous or grayish with violaceous tinge. Gloeocystidia 21—39x8.5—18 µm. Protruding subulate cystidia usually present. Lamprocystidia 11—22(25) x 8—11 µm. Basidia 21—32x4—5.5 µm. Spores allantoid, (6.5)7.5—12 x 2.3—3.3(3.5) µm 09202321300 P. nuda (Fr.: Fr.) Bres. (fig. 6).240
0The collections from Syringa vulgaris differ by adnate, then distinctly reflexed margin. Gloeocystidia are not always well visible, in some old specimens or old basidiomata only rounded empty coats of gloeocystidia (caverns) are found. Similar specimens, collected on Syringa vulgaris, were edited in «Fungi exsiccati suecici, praesertim Upsalienses» (N 1837, coli. Berit et J. Eriksson, 4 May 1948) and «Plantae Norvegicae» (coli. J. St5rdal, 21 September 1951).09202421310N0
0 Basidiomata up to 0.65 mm thick. Hymenial surface pinkish or yellowish. Basidiomata represented by thin, closely adnate part and warts 0.2—0.65 mm thick, with free margin; distinctly layered; basal layer present in some parts. Hyphae of subiculum tightly packed or transformed into pseudoparenchymatic tissue, hyaline to brownish. Gloeocystidia 28—112x9.5—15 µm. Lamprocystidia 15—60 x (7)8—16 µm. Basidia 30—50x5.5—7.5 µm. Spores narrowly ellipsoid, 7.5—9x3.5—4 µm. On Salix sp 010212522330 see P. incarnata.0
0 *. Basidiomata about 0.1 mm thick01021262234011
0 On gymnosperms. Spores 6—7 x (2.3)2.5(3). Lamprocystidia (7.5)8—14(15.5) µm wide. Hymenial surface gray with pinkish or violaceous tinge. Basidiomata consist of brown pseudoparenchymatic tissue. Gloeocystidia 22—44 x (5.5)7—12 µm, of two shapes, fusoid and obtuse. Lamprocystidia 12—37 µm long. Basidia 20—42 x x4—7 µm. Spores allantoid 011262723350 P. pithya (Pers.) J. Erikss. (fig. 5).280
0The characteristic for species dark basal layer in belarusian specimens is absent.011262823360N0
0 *. On angiosperms (Corylus, Populus, Salix). Spores (6.5)7—10(10.5) x (2.3)2.5—3 µm. Lamprocystidia 3.5—7 µm wide. Basidiomata consist of brown pseudoparenchymatic tissue. Gloeocystidia 20—50x6.5—12 µm. Lamprocystidia 11—40 µm long. Protruding subulate cystidia sometimes present. Basidia 16—32 x 3.5—6.5 µm. Spores allantoid 011262923380 P. violaceolivida (Sommerf.) Massee.0
0 Basal layer well developed01210302439013
0 *. Basal layer absent or very thin01210312440015
0 On Fraxinus excelsior. Margin adnate, concolorous with hymenial surface or white and mould-like, then free (but not rolling off), dark brown or blackish. Spores 6—10 x (2)2.3—2.5 µm, allantoid. Lamprocystidia 10—40 x (6)10—14 µm. Basidia 29—34 x 4—7 µm 013303225410 P. limitata (Chaillet ex Fr.: Fr.) Cooke.0
0 *. On other plants (not on Oleaceae). Margin in old basidiomata reflexed to rolling back, not dark brown. Spores 6.5—12.5 x 2.3—3.5 µm01330332542014
0 On Tilia cordata. Hymenial surface gray to brown, with distinct violaceous tinge, ochraceous when dry. Margin firstly adnate, white, mould-like, then concolorous or tan, free. Spores 5.5—11 x2.3—3.5 µm. Abhymenial layer near substrate almost black. Lamprocystidia 27—75x6.5—17.5 µm. Basidia 19—46x3.5—7.5 µm, sometimes present gloeocystidia-like basidioles .. 014333426430 P. rufomarginata (Pers.) Litsch.0
0 *. On Quercus robur, rarely other substrates (Corylus avellana, Frangula alnus). Hymenial surface gray, pinkish gray, ochraceous to pale brown, sometimes cream-coloured. Margin concolorous, free and rolling back (sometimes adnate in young badisiomata). Spores 7.5—12.5 x3—3.5 µm. Abhymenial layer dark brown to blackish. Lamprocystidia 16—50x5—15.5 µm. Subulate protruding cystidia present or not, 48—65 x5—5.5(6) µm. Sometimes present vertically arranged gloeocystidial hyphae, 3—4 µm wide. Basidia 23—40 x 4—7 µm 014333526440 P. quercina (Pers.: Fr.) Cooke (fig. 7).360
0Remarkable form with cream-coloured hymenial surface was collected on Frangula alnus.014333626450N0
0 Rounded caverns or empty coats present 015313727470 see P. nuda.0
0 *. Rounded caverns absent, basidiomata 0.05—0.2 mm thick, effused, cracking, with adnate or slightly free margin. Hymenial surface gray with violaceous tinge, sometimes whitish, ochraceous or browish. Basal layer sometimes present, very thin. Lamprocystidia 18—52(59) x 6—12 µm and smooth protruding cystidia present in the hymenium. Basidia 15—31 x4—6.5 µm. Spores cylindrical or suballantoid, 6.5—11 x  2.3—3(3.3) µm. On different angiosperms, mainly on Betula, Corylus, Frangula, Sorbus, Tilia 015313827480 P. cinerea (Pers.: Fr.) Cooke (fig. 8).0
